  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. The rule is enabled, has already run once, and reports status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has about 14,000 web requests timestamped 2026\u201110\u201104 to 2026\u201112\u201103. Each one records the time, client IP, response code, bytes sent, URL, host, file extension, source and destination country, browser, OS and tags. Only three response codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-de4fcff4 Web logs overview\"** ([open it](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-de4fcff4-web-logs-overview-b49bd98a741d)) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie)\n- Top 10 destination countries\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes\n\nThe default time range is the last 7 days through 30 days ahead, because much of the sample data is dated after today.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-de4fcff4 5xx spike\"** (id `6ca16090-0b4c-4bbf-8727-64de5fee0469`):\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule that checks every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nThe rule has no action connector attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and no notification is sent.",
